[TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswb1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF on the distribution “peer VLAN” (SVI br0.99). Mechanism: dswa1 and dswb1 are advertising OSPF hellos with a /32 netmask on br0.99 while their neighbors (dswa2, dswb2) run the segment as a /28. This causes periodic “NetworkMask mismatch” events; neighbors drop and re-form adjacency, triggering repeated SPF runs and short routing blackouts (“wobbles”).

Localization and evidence:
- dswa2 frr.log repeatedly logs “Packet 10.0.0.79 [Hello:RECV]: NetworkMask mismatch on br0.99:10.0.0.3 (configured prefix length is 28, but hello packet indicates 32).” Router ID 10.0.0.79 = dswa1.
- dswb2 frr.log repeatedly logs the same mismatch with Router ID 10.0.0.81 = dswb1.
- dswa1 br0.99 has 10.0.0.2/28 
[FINAL] stop=no-more-tool-calls tool_calls=39
[SUBMITTED] parsed_ok=True is_anomaly=True faulty_devices=['dswa1', 'dswb1']
[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswb1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF on the distribution “peer VLAN” (SVI br0.99). Mechanism: dswa1 and dswb1 are advertising OSPF hellos with a /32 netmask on br0.99 while their neighbors (dswa2, dswb2) run the segment as a /28. This causes periodic “NetworkMask mismatch” events; neighbors drop and re-form adjacency, triggering repeated SPF runs and short routing blackouts (“wobbles”).

Localization and evidence:
- dswa2 frr.log repeatedly logs “Packet 10.0.0.79 [Hello:RECV]: NetworkMask mismatch on br0.99:10.0.0.3 (configured prefix length is 28, but hello packet indicates 32).” Router ID 10.0.0.79 = dswa1.
- dswb2 frr.log repeatedly logs the same mismatch with Router ID 10.0.0.81 = dswb1.
- dswa1 br0.99 has 10.0.0.2/28 and an extra 10.0.0.1/32 (unnumbered) assigned; FRR shows “inet 10.0.0.1/32 unnumbered” on br0.99. dswb1 br0.99 likewise has 10.0.0.18/28 plus 10.0.0.17/32.
- OSPF neighbor uptimes on the br0.99 peer links are notably low (≈25–27s) compared to other adjacencies, indicating flapping.

Root cause: Stray /32 secondary IPs on br0.99 on dswa1 and dswb1 cause OSPF hellos to intermittently use a /32 mask, which their peers reject, repeatedly resetting the peer adjacencies.

Corrective fix: Remove the /32 secondary IPs from br0.99 on dswa1 (10.0.0.1/32) and dswb1 (10.0.0.17/32), or alternatively set “ip ospf network point-to-point” on br0.99 on all four DSWs so mask is ignored and no DR is elected. Ensure all four br0.99 SVIs use the same /28. This will stabilize the distribution peer adjacencies and stop the campus-wide routing wobbles.
=== END ===